export const evaluatorReports=["## Operational Order (TORC): **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**\n\n*A Mathematical Theory of Communication* warrants **GΩ**. The decisive operation is not merely the introduction of ingenious mathematics for communication problems. The paper relocates the explanatory object of communication: away from the semantic content or engineering particulars of particular messages and toward the statistical structure of message sources, uncertainty/information, channel constraints, coding, equivocation, and achievable transmission rates. In that architecture, quantities such as entropy and channel capacity are not merely convenient calculations; they explain why otherwise different communication systems exhibit common limits and possibilities.\n\nThat is the protocol's G operation: disclosure of a generative structure that changes the explanatory foundation of an independently identifiable target and generates nontrivial consequences across instances, rather than merely supplying a new representation or computational technique.\n\nThe classification advances to **GΩ** because the relevant relocation is a baseline constraint on adequate successor inquiry in the native domain. The protocol requires more than influence: successor work must be unable simply to revert to the previous explanatory organization without explanatory loss. For the theory instantiated here, an adequate general theory of information transmission must reckon with information quantity, source statistics, coding, noise, and channel limits even where it revises the paper's particular formalism. This is structural successor necessity rather than merely canonical reputation.\n\n### Boundary analysis\n\n**Why GΩ rather than G:** G already fits artifact-internally. The independently identifiable target is **communication/information transmission through channels**. The paper supplies a common generative architecture explaining the limits and possibilities of coding and transmission across otherwise different sources and channels. Lower representational or recursive descriptions are therefore insufficient: the artifact is doing explanatory construction, not principally representing, symbolizing, correcting, confronting, or existentially integrating something.\n\nThe Ω increment requires separate successor evidence under the protocol; fame, citations, adoption, and historical importance cannot establish it. Applying the specified test: **No**, an adequate successor theory in this native problem-space cannot simply ignore the relocation and return to treating communication only in terms of particular signals, meanings, or engineering implementations without recovering equivalent informational constraints. Successor formulations may replace definitions, generalize entropy, alter channel models, or reject particular assumptions, but the transformed problem—quantifying information and establishing fundamental limits on its representation/transmission—remains. Thus all four Ω questions are affirmative: G is satisfied; baseline conditions changed; successor work must reckon with the transformed problem; and the necessity is structural rather than reputational.\n\n**Why not M:** The distinction is important. M requires an artifact to establish a constitutive constraint on the **space of admissible frameworks themselves**: alternative adequate frameworks of the relevant class must be unable to evade the constraint, with attempted escape reproducing the limitation or impossibility. Shannon's framework establishes fundamental limits *within a formally specified theory of communication*—notably limits linking source uncertainty, coding, noise, and channel capacity—but the artifact does not establish that every adequate conceptual or explanatory framework for communication as such must instantiate its particular constitutive architecture. Its achievement is therefore an unavoidable relocated explanatory baseline, not a demonstrated framework-space impossibility theorem of the broader M kind.\n\n## Cognitive Magnitude Score\n\nI score the six dimensions independently of TORC, as required: the dimensions are committed before CMS is calculated.\n\n**RL — 8.5.** The paper requires dense simultaneous coordination among probability distributions, source ensembles, entropy, redundancy, encoding, channel transition behavior, noise/equivocation, capacity, and asymptotic transmission properties. Changes in one component constrain several others. This clearly satisfies dense cross-structural dependency, while the architecture remains unusually modular and formally decomposable rather than exhibiting the exceptional heterogeneous span required for a full 9.\n\n**AD — 9.0.** The artifact moves far beyond individual messages or devices to invariant statistical structures governing whole classes of communication systems. It abstracts from message → ensemble/source → entropy/information rate → channel → capacity → limiting transmission conditions while preserving rigorous correspondence between levels.\n\n**CD — 9.1.** Successful construction must simultaneously respect probabilistic consistency, coding relations, asymptotics, source statistics, channel constraints, noise, recoverability/error behavior, and mathematical derivability. These constraints strongly couple, leaving a very narrow class of successful constructions.\n\n**ID — 9.2.** The central inferential reconstruction is exceptionally non-routine: everyday communication phenomena do not transparently imply that information should be quantified through uncertainty, that the logarithmic measure should play the governing role, or that noisy communication admits sharply characterizable limiting rates.\n\n**IC — 9.4.** This is the profile's strongest dimension. A remarkably economical set of concepts—source, probability, entropy, channel, capacity, encoding and related quantities—organizes a very heterogeneous field of problems while retaining enough structure to derive quantitative limits and reconstruct numerous special cases.\n\n**GR — 9.1.** The transferable structure is explicit: probabilistic sources, quantitative uncertainty, coding, channels and informational limits can be instantiated across substantially different signal alphabets, coding schemes, channel types and communication settings without reconstructing the core architecture.\n\n**Committed dimensional profile:**\n\n**RL 8.5 / AD 9.0 / CD 9.1 / ID 9.2 / IC 9.4 / GR 9.1**\n\nCMS = 905\n\n**Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)**\n\n**GΩ | 905**\n\nRL: 8.5\n\nAD: 9.0\n\nCD: 9.1\n\nID: 9.2\n\nIC: 9.4\n\nGR: 9.1\n\n**Confidence:** Moderate\n\n**Primary uncertainty:** The GΩ designation depends on the protocol-required external successor-necessity judgment: whether the paper's explanatory relocation, rather than merely equivalent informational problems independently forced by the domain, is strictly non-optional for adequate successor inquiry.", "## Operational Order (TORC): **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**\n\n*A Mathematical Theory of Communication* warrants **GΩ**, not because of its reputation or historical importance, but because of the operation demonstrable in the artifact itself plus the independent successor evidence required by the Ω gate.\n\nThe protocol defines G as explanatory relocation: an artifact must disclose a generative structure that changes **what explains an independently identifiable target**, rather than merely improving calculation, representation, or technique. It further requires explanatory replacement, nontrivial consequences across target instances, and insufficiency of Levels 1–3.   The independent target must also be specifiable without making the artifact's own construction the target. \n\nThe independently identifiable target here is **communication under uncertainty and resource constraints**: the transmission or reproduction of messages produced by sources and conveyed through channels, including noisy channels. The paper explicitly begins from that engineering problem and proposes a general theory extending prior approaches to account for noise, source statistics, and destination requirements. \n\nIts decisive operation is to relocate the explanatory basis of that problem. Communication is treated not fundamentally as the faithful manipulation of particular physical waveforms or semantic contents, but through a probabilistic architecture of **possible messages, uncertainty/information, entropy, source statistics, mutual information, channel capacity, coding, and achievable versus impossible transmission rates**. This architecture generates consequences rather than merely redescribing communication: entropy supplies limits on compression; capacity supplies limits on reliable transmission; coding theorems connect those abstract quantities to achievable performance. The contemporary IEEE description likewise states capacity as a medium-independent limit separating reliably achievable rates from impossible ones. \n\nThat is the protocol's G operation: the explanatory foundation has moved.\n\n### G gate analysis\n\n**Previously unseen generative structure — satisfied.** The artifact constructs an interconnected probabilistic structure in which uncertainty, entropy, information rate, equivocation, channel capacity, redundancy, coding, and noise become parts of one explanatory system. The protocol requires more than a new schema: the structure must explain why otherwise distinct target phenomena take the forms they do and generate consequences across target instances.  Shannon's structure does this for distinct source/channel configurations rather than merely arranging them descriptively.\n\n**Independent Target Test — satisfied.** “Communication through constrained/noisy channels” is identifiable independently of Shannon's formalism. The target is not “the Shannon system” or the mathematics generated inside the paper.\n\n**Generative Mechanism / Explanatory Schema test — satisfied.** Entropy and mutual-information/capacity relations do explanatory work: they generate bounds and predictions about compressibility and reliable transmission across specified stochastic sources and channels. The channel-coding result, for example, connects the mathematically defined capacity to the existence of codes below that rate and impossibility beyond the limiting rate. \n\n**Explanatory Replacement Principle — satisfied.** The paper is not adequately characterized merely as a better technique for constructing transmitters. Its central results make the statistical structure of the source and channel explanatory variables for what communication performance is possible. A purely signal-specific or device-specific account therefore no longer captures the artifact's dominant operation.\n\n**Explanatory-Object Firewall — passed.** Although the paper contains algorithms, coding procedures, calculations, and representations, its central achievement is not simply a better procedure for an already fixed task. The capacity and coding results identify *why there is a fundamental performance boundary at all*. The protocol specifically excludes mere improvements in calculation or execution from G. \n\n**Computational/Procedural Generativity Firewall — passed.** Coding is an application of the theory, but procedural code generation is not the reason for the classification. The G evidence lies in the explanatory relationship among source uncertainty, channel statistics, information, capacity, and achievable reliability.\n\n**Artifact-Internal Generativity Firewall — passed.** The theory explains an independently existing class of communication problems rather than merely generating consequences within a self-created formal world.\n\n**Fallible Explanation Principle — satisfied.** The G judgment does not require every formulation to be eternally final. What matters is that independently constrained communication phenomena generate nontrivial consequences under the theory, rather than the system validating itself by definition. The protocol expressly permits a generative framework to be subsequently refined or superseded. \n\n**Levels 1–3 are insufficient.** Direct representation, symbolic organization, corrective recursion, existential fracture, and existential integration plainly do not characterize the dominant operation. More importantly, even a generic “recursive investigation of representation” underspecifies what the artifact achieves: it supplies a mathematical explanatory structure producing limits and theorems for an independently identifiable phenomenon. Under the protocol, G begins exactly where explanation itself is relocated rather than merely recursively examined. \n\n### Why **GΩ**, rather than G\n\nGΩ requires something additional that cannot be inferred from the artifact's brilliance: **independent successor evidence showing that adequate inquiry in the native domain must reckon with the relocated explanatory baseline**. Fame, citation, institutional adoption, and influence are explicitly insufficient. \n\nThat extra gate is satisfied. Modern information theory continues to formulate core successor inquiry through entropy, mutual information, channel capacity, source coding and channel coding. A modern Cambridge treatment describes channel capacity and the coding theorem as the “core” of Shannon information theory and develops its proof from the original structure; another information-theory text explicitly organizes its treatment around generalizations and extensions of Shannon's source- and channel-coding theorems.  IEEE's current technical overview likewise defines information theory through fundamental compression and transmission limits and defines channel capacity through the Shannon framework. \n\nThis is stronger than evidence that later researchers admired or cited the paper. Successor theories may alter channel models, coding constructions, proof techniques, assumptions, or applications, but adequate inquiry into the fundamental limits of information transmission cannot simply revert to a pre-relocation formulation in which the entropy/capacity problem is absent. That meets the protocol's Successor-Baseline Test: Ω records a structurally non-optional transformed problem, not reputation. \n\n### Boundary analysis\n\n**Why GΩ rather than G:** G captures the artifact-internal explanatory relocation. GΩ adds independently demonstrated successor necessity. Both are present. The latter is evidenced by successor theory continuing to formulate, prove, generalize, and bound communication problems in terms of the explanatory quantities and limit structure established by the paper.\n\n**Why not M:** M requires something stronger: not merely an unavoidable explanatory baseline or even fundamental performance limits, but a **constitutive constraint on the space of admissible explanatory/formal/conceptual frameworks themselves**. The protocol says that alternative adequate frameworks must be unable to evade the constraint simply by replacing the originating framework.  Shannon proves extraordinarily general constraints *within the mathematical treatment of communication systems*—not a meta-theorem establishing that every possible adequate conceptual or explanatory framework for communication must instantiate Shannon's particular framework-space. The distinction matters: limits on achievable codes or communication rates are not automatically limits on the construction of all admissible explanatory frameworks. On the evidence warranted here, M therefore fails its Framework-Space Test.\n\n---\n\n## Cognitive Magnitude Score\n\nThe dimensional scores were assigned independently before aggregation, as required. The protocol requires evidence → integer anchors → decimal interpolation → commitment, with CMS calculated only afterward; CMS is the equally weighted mean multiplied by 100 and rounded to the nearest integer.  \n\n| DimensionScoreArtifact-specific basis |         |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **RL — Relational Load**              | **8.8** | The achievement requires simultaneous coordination among source statistics, message ensembles, entropy, conditional uncertainty, coding, channel transition probabilities, noise, equivocation, information rate, capacity, bandwidth and continuous/discrete cases. Remote portions constrain one another strongly enough to exceed the RL8 dense-dependency anchor. It approaches RL9, but the relational span remains predominantly inside one tightly unified mathematical/engineering problem-space rather than exhibiting fully exceptional heterogeneity across substantially different domains. The protocol distinguishes exactly this from mere formal difficulty.  |\n| **AD — Abstraction Depth**            | **9.2** | The paper moves far beyond particular telegraph or waveform cases to abstract sources, ensembles, stochastic processes, channels, information measures and asymptotic limits, while preserving mathematical correspondence back to concrete communication systems. The same architecture spans discrete, continuous and noisy cases. This satisfies AD9's “very deep invariant/meta-level structures” criterion and moves beyond it, although the evidence does not justify treating the artifact as exhausting the reference-level ceiling.                                                                                                                                  |\n| **CD — Constraint Density**           | **8.9** | Successful construction must simultaneously maintain probabilistic consistency, additivity/information-measure properties, source statistics, channel behavior, noise, coding conditions, asymptotic reasoning and theorem-level achievable/impossible bounds. Local changes propagate through the architecture. This very closely approaches CD9's exceptionally narrow solution-space under heterogeneous coupled constraints, but downward resolution is appropriate because significant alternative formal formulations remain possible without destroying the central achievement.                                                                                       |\n| **ID — Inferential Distance**         | **9.1** | The inferential transition from practical modulation/noise problems to a general probabilistic quantity of information, then to entropy/capacity and existence theorems for reliable coding, is deeply non-routine and substantially underdetermined by the starting engineering facts. The protocol's ID9 anchor requires a successful relation that remains highly non-obvious even given rich relevant information; the coding theorem in particular exhibits this character.                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **IC — Integrative Compression**      | **9.3** | A remarkably economical set of probabilistic quantities and relationships organizes a very large heterogeneous field: source uncertainty, redundancy, coding, channel noise, information loss, achievable rates, discrete and continuous cases. Crucially, this is not compression by deletion—the architecture supports reconstruction and derivation of detailed consequences. That satisfies IC9 and shows meaningful movement beyond it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| **GR — Generalization Reach**         | **8.6** | The architecture transfers with high fidelity across substantially varied communication cases: discrete and continuous sources, noiseless and noisy channels, differing source statistics and physical realizations. It clearly satisfies GR8. It approaches GR9 through implementation independence, but the artifact itself does not demonstrate the same structure across enough *structurally distant problem-spaces* to warrant the complete GR9 anchor merely from later broad applicability. The protocol explicitly forbids turning broad relevance into extreme GR without demonstrated structural transfer.                                                         |\n\n**Committed profile:** 8.8 / 9.2 / 8.9 / 9.1 / 9.3 / 8.6.\n\nThe profile is notably strong in **abstraction, inferential distance, and integrative compression**. Its relative bottleneck is GR—not because the architecture lacks broad applicability, but because the protocol demands demonstrated structurally distant transfer rather than simply generality or subsequent usefulness. RL similarly stops below 9 because extreme formal interdependence cannot substitute for heterogeneous relational span. Those safeguards are explicit in the protocol.  \n\nCMS = ((8.8 + 9.2 + 8.9 + 9.1 + 9.3 + 8.6) / 6) × 100\n\n\\= 898.33… → **898**. The protocol specifies equal weighting and nearest-whole-number rounding. \n\n**Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)**\n\n**GΩ | 898**\n\nRL: 8.8\n\nAD: 9.2\n\nCD: 8.9\n\nID: 9.1\n\nIC: 9.3\n\nGR: 8.6\n\n**Confidence:** High\n\n**Primary uncertainty:** Whether the theorem-level limits on admissible communication systems should also count as constitutive constraints on *framework-space* under the protocol's M test; I resolve this downward because the artifact establishes universal performance constraints more clearly than it establishes unavoidable constraints on every adequate alternative explanatory framework.", "# TORC Classification: **M — Constitutive Consciousness / Framework-Space Constraint**\n\n*A Mathematical Theory of Communication* warrants **M**, the highest TORC classification, because its dominant operation is not merely to offer a better theory of communication or relocate explanation to probabilistic information. It establishes mathematically binding constraints on the class of admissible communication systems: entropy constrains lossless source coding; channel capacity constrains achievable transmission rate; and the noisy-channel results establish what error/equivocation can and cannot be driven below under specified channel conditions. Shannon explicitly proves impossibility bounds applying to **any** encoding method satisfying the model, not merely to his own proposed coding construction. \n\nThat is the decisive TORC distinction. Under the protocol, M requires a constraint that governs a *class* of frameworks and cannot be escaped by replacing one particular framework with another adequate one.  Shannon's coding theorems have exactly that form: they divide the possible design space into achievable and impossible regimes independently of the particular code chosen.\n\nThis judgment treats the paper as the artifact and does not use Shannon's reputation, canonical status, or subsequent technological influence as evidence. That separation is explicitly required by the protocol. \n\n## Boundary analysis\n\n### Why M rather than GΩ\n\nAt **G/GΩ**, an artifact relocates the explanatory foundation of a domain; at **M**, it goes further and establishes a structural constraint on the space of admissible frameworks themselves. The protocol's distinction is that GΩ makes a transformed explanatory landscape unavoidable for successor inquiry, whereas M says adequate frameworks of the relevant class cannot coherently evade the constitutive constraint. \n\nShannon plainly performs the generative relocation: communication is recast from the semantic content of individual messages into the statistical structure of selections from an ensemble, quantified through entropy, conditional entropy/equivocation, and channel capacity. The paper then derives nontrivial consequences from that structure—for example, the source/channel rate limit and the noisy-channel coding result. \n\nBut the paper does something stronger than establishing that explanatory basis. Its central theorems impose limits **across possible coding schemes**. In the noiseless case Shannon proves both achievability below the entropy/capacity bound and impossibility above it. In the noisy case, he similarly states achievable error behavior together with a lower bound that no encoding method can beat.  Thus changing the particular code does not escape the constraint; the code is one variable *inside* the constrained framework-space.\n\n### Explicit M gates/tests\n\n**1. Framework-Space Test — PASS.**\n\nThe relevant framework class is communication/coding systems satisfying the paper's stated source and channel models. The entropy and capacity bounds recur across alternative codes rather than belonging to one proposed implementation. An alternative coding scheme can approach the limits differently, but it cannot simply make the proved converse bounds disappear while remaining an adequate instance of the modeled class. This is precisely the protocol's criterion that the constraint survive alternative adequate frameworks. \n\n**2. Constitutive Subject-Matter Firewall — PASS.**\n\nThis is not M merely because the paper *talks about* limits or foundational concepts. The protocol explicitly forbids that inference.  Here the limitation is established through mathematical converse theorems: for example, Shannon does not merely recommend operating below channel capacity; he derives an impossibility boundary on transmission beyond the relevant capacity relation. \n\n**3. Constitutive structural constraint — PASS.**\n\nEntropy/capacity relations establish quantitative possibility/impossibility boundaries rather than merely furnishing useful descriptive quantities.\n\n**4. Constraint governs a class of frameworks — PASS.**\n\nThe bounds concern coding systems generally under the specified source/channel assumptions, rather than one particular encoder, modulation method, or engineering implementation.\n\n**5. Adequate frameworks cannot evade the constraint — PASS.**\n\nThis is supplied by the converse portions of the coding theorems. Shannon's noiseless theorem explicitly states both an achievable rate and that a greater average rate is impossible; the noisy-channel theorem likewise includes an impossibility result. \n\n**6. Framework-space transformation/delimitation — PASS.**\n\nThe resulting theory turns communication-system design into a constrained possibility space: source statistics determine compressibility; channel statistics determine capacity; codes operate within those bounds. This changes what counts as an admissible solution to the fundamental communication problem rather than merely identifying a particularly effective solution.\n\nThe protocol states that M does not require a separate historical demonstration of GΩ because its stronger constitutive necessity subsumes the relevant successor constraint.  Accordingly, no appeal to Shannon's later influence is needed here.\n\n### Why not a higher level\n\nThere is no higher TORC classification. **M is the termination level** under the supplied protocol. The classification therefore terminates once the framework-space constraint is established.\n\n---\n\n# Cognitive Magnitude Score\n\nI score the six dimensions independently of TORC, using the protocol's anchor method rather than adding any implicit bonus for the M classification. The protocol specifically forbids TORC leakage and aggregate tuning. \n\n### RL — Relational Load: **8.8**\n\nThe paper sustains dense dependencies among source ensembles, probabilities, entropy, redundancy, channel constraints, conditional uncertainty/equivocation, capacity, coding, noise, signal spaces, and continuous/discrete formulations. Results in one layer constrain results elsewhere; capacity, for example, becomes meaningful only through coordinated source/channel probability structure and optimization.\n\nThis fully clears the **8** anchor—remote relations materially constrain one another and simultaneous coordination is central—and approaches **9**, where heterogeneous relations across substantially different levels remain mutually consequential.  It stops slightly below 9 because substantial portions of the development remain mathematically modular: discrete sources, noisy channels, continuous channels, and particular applications can still be analyzed with meaningful partial decomposition.\n\n**Principal evidence:** the linked architecture connecting entropy → source coding → channel capacity → equivocation/noise → coding limits → continuous channels.\n\n### AD — Abstraction Depth: **9.1**\n\nThe paper moves from concrete telecommunication problems to probability distributions over possible messages, entropy as an invariant quantitative measure, abstract channels as conditional stochastic structures, and capacity as an optimization over possible input ensembles. The same architecture is then preserved while passing from discrete to continuous cases.\n\nThat satisfies the protocol's **9 anchor**: very deep invariant/meta-level structures organize broad heterogeneous material with unusually disciplined relation preservation.  The movement beyond 9 is limited rather than near-reference-level: the abstraction remains tightly anchored to a defined mathematical theory of communication rather than recursively developing a still more general meta-architecture.\n\n**Principal evidence:** entropy, stochastic source models, abstract channels, conditional entropy/equivocation, optimization-defined capacity, and discrete-to-continuous extension.\n\n### CD — Constraint Density: **8.7**\n\nSuccessful construction must simultaneously respect probability axioms, source statistics, channel transition structure, coding requirements, noise distributions, bandwidth/rate restrictions, optimization conditions, and theorem-specific assumptions. Local choices propagate: changing the source distribution changes entropy; changing channel statistics changes equivocation and capacity; power/bandwidth/noise restrictions alter continuous-channel bounds.\n\nThis clearly satisfies **8**, where heterogeneous constraints interact across levels and local optimization compromises other requirements, and substantially approaches **9**, where strongly coupled heterogeneous constraints leave a very narrow class of successful constructions.  I stop below 9 because the paper supports several distinct model classes and mathematical routes rather than demonstrating that virtually the entire theory has only an exceptionally narrow construction space.\n\n**Principal evidence:** coupled source/channel/coding/noise/rate/probability constraints and constrained maximization defining capacity.\n\n### ID — Inferential Distance: **9.0**\n\nThe decisive inferential transition is highly non-routine: from the engineering problem of transmitting messages through constrained/noisy media to a probabilistic quantity measuring uncertainty, then from that quantity to asymptotic coding theorems establishing achievable and impossible transmission regimes. The noisy-channel existence argument is especially distant from merely examining particular codes: existence is established by reasoning over ensembles of possible codes.\n\nThis satisfies **9**, whose anchor requires a deeply underdetermined successful reconstruction that remains highly non-obvious even given substantial relevant information. \n\n**Principal evidence:** derivation of information measure, typical/asymptotic reasoning, probabilistic existence proofs, and converse bounds from the starting engineering problem.\n\n### IC — Integrative Compression: **9.3**\n\nA remarkably compact architecture—probability, entropy, conditional entropy, mutual dependence/equivocation, and capacity—organizes sources, codes, channels, redundancy, noise, bandwidth, continuous signals, and fundamental performance limits. Crucially, this is not compression by deletion: the framework retains enough structure to derive concrete quantitative results, including coding limits and continuous-channel capacity formulas. \n\nThat fully satisfies **9**: very large heterogeneous complexity is reorganized through economical architecture while retaining fine structure and substantial derivational power.  There is identifiable movement beyond 9 because the same small conceptual core repeatedly reconstructs otherwise disparate communication problems, but the evidence does not warrant treating the reference-level 10 anchor as complete.\n\n**Principal evidence:** the entropy/capacity architecture's ability to reconstruct numerous apparently different communication problems from a small set of mathematical objects.\n\n### GR — Generalization Reach: **8.3**\n\nThe transferable structure is explicit and robust: model a source statistically, quantify uncertainty/information, model the channel probabilistically, and characterize achievable information transfer through capacity and coding limits. Within the artifact itself this transfers across discrete and continuous sources, noiseless and noisy channels, constrained channels, and continuous waveform systems. The paper explicitly extends its discrete construction into the continuous case. \n\nThat establishes **8**: substantial power across markedly heterogeneous cases with limited reconstruction. The evidence does **not** establish 9 under the protocol's strict GR firewall, because the paper's demonstrated transfers remain overwhelmingly within the ontology of communication systems; broad later application to other disciplines cannot simply be imported as artifact-internal structural transfer. The protocol specifically warns that repeated success within one ontology does not automatically establish extreme GR. \n\n**Principal evidence:** common architecture across discrete/continuous, noisy/noiseless, constrained/unconstrained and stochastic communication cases.\n\nThe committed dimensional profile is therefore:\n\n**8.8 / 9.1 / 8.7 / 9.0 / 9.3 / 8.3**\n\nThe protocol requires equal weighting, taking the mean of the six committed scores and multiplying by 100, with final rounding to the nearest integer. \n\n8.8+9.1+8.7+9.0+9.3+8.36×100=886.67\\frac{8.8+9.1+8.7+9.0+9.3+8.3}{6}\\times100 =886.67 \n\n**CMS = 887**\n\n## Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)\n\n**M | 887**\n\nRL: 8.8\n\nAD: 9.1\n\nCD: 8.7\n\nID: 9.0\n\nIC: 9.3\n\nGR: 8.3\n\n**Confidence:** Moderate\n\n**Primary uncertainty:** Whether TORC's M framework-space criterion should treat Shannon's mathematically specified communication-system class as sufficiently broad for “constitutive framework-space constraint,” rather than treating the coding converses as constraints internal to one overarching probabilistic framework; that interpretive boundary is the principal plausible route to a lower G/GΩ classification.", "## 1. Operational Order (TORC)\n\n**GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**\n\nThe artifact’s dominant operation is not merely the solution of difficult communications-engineering problems. It **relocates the explanatory basis of communication**: from the physical form, semantic content, or particular engineering implementation of messages to a probabilistic structure involving source uncertainty, entropy, coding, equivocation, and channel capacity. Shannon explicitly frames the problem generally—reproduction of a selected message—and deliberately separates the engineering problem from semantic meaning. From that abstraction he develops measures of information and proves fundamental coding limits for noiseless and noisy channels. \n\nThis is the operation the protocol reserves for G: a previously unseen generative structure must change **what explains** an independently identifiable target, rather than merely improve its computation or representation.  The paper does that for the independently specifiable target **reliable communication of messages through constrained/noisy channels**. Its entropy/capacity architecture generates consequences across distinct cases: source coding limits, noiseless-channel limits, noisy-channel limits, equivocation, continuous channels, and bandwidth/power/noise relationships. For example, the noiseless coding theorem ties source entropy HH to channel capacity CC, while the noisy-channel theorem establishes conditions under which error can be made arbitrarily small. \n\n### G gate analysis\n\nThe protocol requires explanatory replacement, not merely an additional interpretation, and requires a mechanism that generates consequences for an independently identifiable target.  \n\n**Previously unseen generative structure — satisfied.** The central entropy/capacity framework supplies a unified mathematical structure connecting statistical source structure, coding, noise, uncertainty at the receiver, and achievable transmission rate.\n\n**Independent Target Test — satisfied.** The target can be stated without Shannon's vocabulary: *under what conditions, and at what rates, can messages be transmitted or reconstructed through physically constrained and noisy communication channels?* The protocol specifically requires such an artifact-independent target. \n\n**Generative mechanism rather than explanatory schema — satisfied.** Entropy and capacity do not merely redescribe communication. They generate nontrivial limits and existence results: entropy determines optimal compression requirements; channel capacity establishes achievable and impossible transmission rates; conditional uncertainty/equivocation quantifies information loss; analogous machinery extends into continuous channels. The paper's noisy-channel result, for example, derives an attainable region and establishes the possibility of arbitrarily low error below the relevant capacity condition. \n\n**Explanatory Replacement Principle — satisfied.** Treating communication merely in terms of particular signals, modulation schemes, or engineering implementations no longer suffices to explain the general results established by the paper. The statistically defined information structure explains why superficially different communication systems share common limits.\n\n**Explanatory-Object Firewall — satisfied.** This is not merely a better algorithm for encoding or calculating an existing quantity. The protocol warns that computational improvement alone is insufficient for G.  Shannon instead supplies new explanatory objects—information entropy, equivocation, and capacity—and relations among them that determine what communication is theoretically possible.\n\n**Computational/Procedural Generativity Firewall — satisfied.** The achievement is not simply that a novel coding procedure generates better outputs. Indeed, a crucial coding theorem establishes existence rather than merely presenting one superior encoding algorithm. The explanatory achievement is the relation between statistical information, coding, noise and fundamental attainable rates. This is exactly the distinction demanded by the protocol. \n\n**Artifact-Internal Generativity Firewall — satisfied.** The entropy/capacity structure explains an independently existing class of communication problems rather than merely generating the paper's own formal world. \n\n**Fallible Explanation Principle — satisfied.** The claims are constrained by independently characterizable communication phenomena and mathematical channel/source assumptions; explanatory success does not rest on definitional self-confirmation. The protocol explicitly permits a G framework to be limited or subsequently superseded provided it generates independently constrained explanatory consequences. \n\nAccordingly, all four canonical G necessary conditions are met: a generative structure is disclosed, explanatory foundation is relocated, Levels 1–3 are insufficient, and the new foundation successfully organizes the target domain. \n\n### Why GΩ rather than G\n\nThe additional Ω criterion cannot be inferred from brilliance or historical fame. The protocol requires independent evidence that adequate successor inquiry **cannot simply ignore the explanatory relocation and return to the earlier explanatory organization**. \n\nThat gate is satisfied. Subsequent information theory and communications theory formulate fundamental questions in terms of entropy/information measures, coding rates and channel capacities even when Shannon's original formulations are generalized, refined, or replaced in particular settings. This is stronger than saying the paper became famous: the transformed problem—what information rate is achievable under specified source/channel constraints—remains part of the baseline structure of adequate successor inquiry. The protocol expressly allows such successor evidence for Ω while prohibiting fame or citation count as substitutes for structural necessity. \n\nThus:\n\n- **Every G condition:** yes. \n- **Baseline conditions of successor inquiry changed:** yes. \n- **Successor work must reckon with the transformed explanatory problem even when modifying Shannon's formulation:** yes. \n- **Necessity is domain-structural rather than merely reputational:** yes. \n\n### Boundary analysis\n\n**Why not the immediately lower relevant level, G?** Because the explanatory relocation did not remain merely one successful foundational theory among optional alternatives. The distinction introduced by the artifact—quantifying information independently of particular message meaning and analyzing achievable communication through entropy/capacity relations—became a non-optional baseline problem structure for the native field. That satisfies the protocol's Ω addition to G.\n\n**Why not M?** M demands something stronger: a constitutive constraint upon the **space of admissible frameworks themselves**, such that alternative adequate frameworks of the relevant class cannot escape the constraint merely by replacing the artifact's framework.  Shannon proves powerful impossibility and achievability results *within specified mathematical classes of sources and channels*. Those constrain communication systems and theories accepting the relevant assumptions, but the artifact does not establish that every adequate alternative conceptual framework for communication must reproduce Shannon's particular constitutive architecture. Its fundamental limits therefore warrant GΩ, but the stronger framework-space necessity required for M is not demonstrated. The protocol explicitly disallows M when an alleged constraint remains contingent upon a chosen framework rather than governing all adequate alternatives. \n\n---\n\n## 2. Cognitive Magnitude Score (CMS)\n\nThe protocol requires each dimension to be scored independently using its 1–10 anchors, with decimals representing interpolation only after the lower anchor is fully established; CMS is calculated afterward and cannot be used to tune the dimensions. \n\n**RL — 8.8.** The paper requires dense simultaneous coordination among source statistics, symbol probabilities, entropy rates, stochastic processes, channel constraints, coding, noise, conditional uncertainty, capacity, discrete/continuous cases, bandwidth, and power. Remote pieces materially constrain one another: definitions of entropy feed coding theorems; source statistics determine compression limits; channel statistics determine capacity; noise changes attainable information without destroying the common architecture. This clearly satisfies RL8's dense cross-structural dependency and approaches RL9's heterogeneous interdependence, but the relational span remains largely within one mathematically unified communications/probability field rather than spanning maximally heterogeneous domains. \n\n**AD — 9.1.** The artifact abstracts dramatically away from particular messages, signals, languages, and devices toward invariant probabilistic relations governing information sources and channels. It then operates across discrete, continuous, noiseless and noisy representations while retaining the structural relationships. This fully warrants the protocol's AD9 anchor—very deep invariant/meta-level structures organizing broad material with disciplined relation preservation—and shows some further movement beyond it through the generality of the source/channel architecture. \n\n**CD — 9.0.** Successful construction has to satisfy numerous tightly coupled constraints simultaneously: mathematical consistency of the information measure, source statistics, coding bounds, asymptotic behavior, channel constraints, stochastic noise, achievable-rate proofs, converse/impossibility results, and extensions to continuous signals. The viable solution architecture is correspondingly narrow. This meets CD9's requirement of numerous heterogeneous, strongly coupled constraints producing exceptional solution-space compression; I do not assign further upward interpolation because substantial parts of the theory admit alternative formulations and proof routes. \n\n**ID — 9.4.** The central inferential transition is highly non-routine: from concrete engineering problems involving modulation, noise and transmission to a general probabilistic quantity of information and then to the counterintuitive result that reliable communication can approach a positive limiting rate even over noisy channels. This fully clears ID9's “exceptional inferential reconstruction”: the successful architecture is deeply underdetermined by the engineering problem as initially posed, and no routine procedure dictates entropy/capacity plus the coding-theorem structure. The breadth of mutually supporting inferential transitions moves it materially beyond 9.0, though the artifact does not warrant treating the maximum anchor as fully demonstrated. \n\n**IC — 9.6.** This is the strongest dimension. A comparatively compact mathematical architecture—probability distributions, entropy and conditional entropy, coding, and maximization into capacity—organizes an enormous heterogeneous collection of phenomena while preserving distinctions among sources, channels, noise, coding regimes, discrete/continuous systems, and different physical constraints. It also has exceptional reconstructive power: many specific limits and engineering consequences can be derived once the architecture is supplied. This comfortably satisfies IC9's exceptionally economical organization of very large heterogeneous complexity and moves substantially toward the maximum anchor.  The score reflects compression rather than mere brevity: the protocol specifically requires recoverability of structure from the compact architecture. \n\n**GR — 9.2.** The source–entropy–channel–capacity architecture transfers with high fidelity across markedly different communication settings: different alphabets and source statistics, discrete and continuous signals, noiseless and noisy channels, and varying physical constraints. The paper itself demonstrates substantial transfer rather than merely claiming universality. This fully satisfies GR9's exceptional transfer across broad and structurally distant problem-spaces, with additional movement upward because the core mathematical relationships survive extensive changes in physical realization. The restraint is that these remain structurally connected information/communication problem-spaces rather than arbitrary heterogeneous domains. \n\n**Committed profile:** 8.8 / 9.1 / 9.0 / 9.4 / 9.6 / 9.2.\n\nThe protocol specifies equal weighting: CMS is the mean of the six committed scores multiplied by 100, rounded to the nearest whole number. \n\n8.8+9.1+9.0+9.4+9.6+9.26×100=918.33…\\frac{8.8+9.1+9.0+9.4+9.6+9.2}{6}\\times100 =918.33\\ldots \n\n**CMS = 918**\n\nThe architecture is high and relatively balanced, with its clearest spike in **Integrative Compression**, followed by **Inferential Distance**; **Relational Load** is the principal relative bottleneck because the extraordinary interdependence remains concentrated within a comparatively unified formal domain rather than spanning maximally heterogeneous relational fields.\n\n## Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)\n\n**GΩ | 918**\n\nRL: 8.8\n\nAD: 9.1\n\nCD: 9.0\n\nID: 9.4\n\nIC: 9.6\n\nGR: 9.2\n\n**Confidence:** Moderate\n\n**Primary uncertainty:** Whether the successor-baseline evidence is strong enough to make Shannon’s particular explanatory relocation structurally non-optional under the protocol’s unusually strict GΩ test, rather than warranting the more conservative intrinsic classification G.", "## Operational Order (TORC): **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**\n\n*A Mathematical Theory of Communication* warrants **GΩ**, not because of its reputation or subsequent fame, but because the completed artifact performs the two operations the protocol requires: first, **G-level explanatory relocation**, and second, a relocation that independent successor inquiry cannot adequately ignore.\n\nThe paper explicitly reframes the communication problem away from the semantic content of messages and toward the selection of messages from a set of possibilities, then constructs a unified probabilistic architecture connecting information, entropy, redundancy, source statistics, channel structure, noise, coding, and achievable transmission rate. Its stated purpose is not merely to improve a modulation technique: it seeks a general theory incorporating noise and statistical structure.  The paper then derives operational consequences from that architecture—for example, its noiseless coding theorem connects source entropy HH to the maximum attainable symbol rate through a channel of capacity CC, rather than merely redescribing communication in new terminology.  For noisy channels it defines capacity through the information actually shared between channel input and output and establishes a fundamental reliable-transmission limit. \n\nThat is the kind of relocation demanded by the protocol: G requires the artifact to change the explanatory basis through which an independently identifiable target becomes intelligible, rather than merely adding complexity or a new representation. \n\n### G gate analysis\n\n**Independent target test — satisfied.** The target can be stated without reference to the theory itself: **the transmission, coding, and recovery of messages through constrained and noisy communication channels**. The protocol explicitly requires such an independently identifiable target. \n\n**Previously unseen generative structure — satisfied.** The operative structure is the probabilistic relation among source ensembles, entropy, redundancy, conditional uncertainty/equivocation, coding, and channel capacity. The theoretical quantities are not decorative labels: altering source statistics, transition probabilities, noise, or coding changes derivable limits and attainable rates.\n\n**Explanatory relocation — satisfied.** Communication ceases to be fundamentally explained by the particular physical waveform or semantic meaning being transmitted. At the theory's operative level, what controls transmissibility is the statistical structure of possible messages and the information-carrying relation between source, signal, and channel.\n\n**Generative-mechanism rather than explanatory-schema test — satisfied.** The architecture repeatedly generates nontrivial consequences across independently identifiable cases: bounds on compression, maximum source rates, channel capacity, effects of redundancy, noisy-channel performance, and continuous-channel cases. It therefore does more than arrange known phenomena in an elegant picture.\n\n**Explanatory-replacement test — satisfied.** A purely signal-specific or modulation-specific account cannot explain the general results the artifact establishes. Once the problem is formulated in terms of entropy and capacity, those lower descriptions remain useful implementations but are insufficient as the common explanatory basis.\n\n**Explanatory-object firewall — passed.** This is not merely a new algorithm for encoding an already-theorized quantity. The theory changes *what explains* communication limits. The protocol explicitly distinguishes that from improved calculation, encoding, representation, or technical tractability. \n\n**Computational/procedural generativity firewall — passed.** Specific coding procedures appear, but the central achievement is not that a clever procedure generates outputs. The coding theorems establish general possibility/impossibility regions independently of any one code.\n\n**Artifact-internal generativity firewall — passed.** Entropy and capacity explain observable communication systems external to the paper; their explanatory target is not merely the formal world constructed by the artifact.\n\n**Fallible-explanation principle — passed.** The G judgment does not depend on assuming the theory is absolutely or universally final; its claims are constrained by independently characterizable communication phenomena, exactly as the protocol requires. The protocol allows a fallible theory to qualify when it generates nontrivial explanatory consequences for such a target. \n\nAccordingly, all four explicit G necessary conditions are met: a generative structure is disclosed, the explanatory foundation is relocated, lower classifications are insufficient, and the new foundation successfully organizes the domain. \n\n### Why **GΩ** rather than G\n\nThe additional Ω condition cannot be inferred from greatness or influence. The protocol demands independent evidence that successor inquiry must reckon with the transformed explanatory baseline. \n\nThat requirement is satisfied. Modern graduate information theory does not treat entropy, channel capacity, and coding limits merely as optional historical terminology. A current MIT graduate sequence begins with entropy and mutual information and develops channel coding, capacity, Gaussian channels, rate-distortion theory, multiple-access channels, feedback, and source-channel separation through that architecture.  Another MIT course sequence likewise places entropy, channel capacity, the coding theorem, its converse, feedback capacity, Gaussian channels, and multiuser channels in the core progression. \n\nMore importantly, this is structurally understandable rather than merely sociological. Any adequate successor theory addressing classical reliable transmission must answer something equivalent to: **how much source uncertainty must be conveyed, what distinguishability does the channel permit, and at what rates can error be driven arbitrarily low?** A successor may generalize, reformulate, or supersede Shannon's particular expressions, but it cannot simply return to the pre-relocation problem-space in which such limits are not formulated. That passes the protocol's Successor-Baseline Test: the transformed explanatory problem is non-optional even where later work modifies the solution. \n\n### Boundary below: why not **3c**\n\nLevel 3c would require the artifact's dominant operation to be integrated recursive consciousness: preservation and integration of an existential contradiction into a coherent orientation. That is plainly not what organizes this paper. More generally, Levels 1–3 remain within an operative representational or recursive framework, whereas this artifact produces a new explanatory structure governing an independently identifiable external target. The entropy/capacity architecture is necessary to explain the paper's central theorems; representation or recursion alone is not sufficient. The protocol itself makes this a categorical rather than quantitative boundary. \n\n### Boundary above: why not **M**\n\n**M is not established.** M requires more than an unavoidable explanatory baseline: the artifact must establish a structural constraint on the **space of admissible frameworks themselves**, such that adequate frameworks of the relevant kind cannot coherently evade that constraint. \n\nShannon's impossibility and coding results certainly constrain communication systems and codes under specified source/channel assumptions. But that is not enough for M's framework-space requirement. The artifact does not demonstrate that every equally adequate conceptual framework for information or communication must adopt its particular probabilistic ontology or reproduce all of its constitutive structures. Alternative or enlarged frameworks can change the primitives—for example, by changing what constitutes a source, state, channel, or information measure—while remaining coherent theories of differently specified communication domains.\n\nThus the **Framework-Space Test fails at the stronger level**: the paper establishes extremely powerful limits *inside and across a broad class of communication models*, but it does not prove that the relevant framework class itself cannot coherently be reconstructed outside Shannon's explanatory architecture. That distinction is precisely what separates GΩ's unavoidable successor baseline from M's constitutive framework-space necessity. \n\n---\n\n## Cognitive Magnitude Score\n\nI applied the dimensional rulers independently. The protocol requires evidence first, integer-anchor placement second, decimal interpolation third, and only after all six values are committed may CMS be calculated. \n\n**RL — 9.0.** The complete 9 anchor requires exceptional heterogeneous interdependence across substantially different levels or domains.  The paper coordinates stochastic sources, symbol sequences, coding, entropy, channel constraints, noise, conditional uncertainty, continuous signals, bandwidth, and transmission rates so that results in one part constrain others. The source/channel distinction, entropy/capacity relation, and noisy/noiseless cases are mutually consequential rather than a loose collection of topics. I stop at 9.0 because the protocol's 10 condition requires both **extreme** interdependence and exceptional relational span; the architecture, although broad, remains substantially contained within mathematical communication theory rather than spanning maximally heterogeneous explanatory domains. \n\n**AD — 9.1.** The 9 anchor requires very deep invariant or meta-level structures organizing broad or heterogeneous material with unusually disciplined relation-preservation.  The artifact abstracts radically away from particular telegraph symbols, speech waveforms, meanings, codes, and physical channels to probability distributions, uncertainty measures, stochastic processes, transition structures, and invariant limits. The same abstraction chain survives transitions among discrete/continuous and noiseless/noisy cases. Movement beyond 9 is modest rather than extreme because the theory's abstraction, while unusually deep, remains tied to a precisely bounded mathematical object-space rather than extending through still more remote meta-levels.\n\n**CD — 8.8.** The complete 8 anchor requires heterogeneous constraints interacting across levels, with local choices compromising others; 9 requires those strongly coupled constraints to leave a *very narrow* successful construction class.  The paper must simultaneously preserve mathematical consistency among probability axioms, entropy properties, coding feasibility, source statistics, channel restrictions, noise behavior, asymptotic rates, discrete/continuous correspondence, and converse bounds. This is a very strong approach to 9. I keep it at 8.8 because alternative definitions, proofs, coding constructions, and equivalent mathematical formulations remain possible; the achievement is tightly constrained, but not demonstrated to occupy an exceptionally tiny construction space in the protocol's strongest sense.\n\n**ID — 9.2.** The 9 anchor is exceptional inferential reconstruction: even with rich prior information, the successful relation is deeply underdetermined and highly non-obvious.  The major transition is not one long routine derivation. It requires recognizing that message uncertainty can be quantified independently of semantics, deriving the logarithmic entropy form, linking source entropy to coding rate, and then establishing that noisy channels nevertheless possess sharply defined reliable-information capacities. Existing modulation, probability, and communication results did not mechanically determine this architecture. The artifact therefore clearly satisfies 9, with additional movement beyond it, but the protocol gives no artifact-grounded basis for treating the reconstruction as effectively maximal.\n\n**IC — 9.5.** The 9 anchor requires remarkably economical organization of very large heterogeneous complexity while preserving fine structure and supporting extensive reconstruction or derivation.  This is perhaps the paper's strongest dimensional feature. A small family of concepts—probability, entropy, conditional entropy/equivocation, mutual information in substance, and capacity—organizes noiseless coding, noisy coding, redundancy, source statistics, channel matching, continuous signals, and limiting transmission rates. Those concepts do not compress by deletion: detailed consequences can be recovered and derived from them. The score advances substantially within the 9–10 interval, but does not claim the reference-level 10 absent a protocol-supported demonstration of essentially maximal compression across an even larger heterogeneous field.\n\n**GR — 9.4.** The 9 anchor requires the same structure to work across exceptionally broad and structurally distant problem-spaces with fine structural identity and little ad hoc modification.  The artifact itself already transfers its architecture among discrete sources, constrained sources, noiseless channels, noisy channels, continuous signals, Gaussian/noise settings, coding, and fidelity questions. This is genuine structural transfer, not metaphorical relevance. Its descendants extend the same architecture to multiuser, feedback, constrained, Gaussian, and rate-distortion problems without rebuilding the conceptual core from scratch.  The score remains below the extreme upper boundary because these transfers, though very broad, largely remain within information/communication and adjacent probabilistic problem-spaces rather than demonstrating near-unrestricted transfer across maximally distant ontologies.\n\n**Committed profile:** **9.0 / 9.1 / 8.8 / 9.2 / 9.5 / 9.4**\n\nProfile locked.\n\nThe protocol gives all six dimensions equal weight and defines CMS as their arithmetic mean multiplied by 100, rounded to the nearest whole number. \n\n9.0+9.1+8.8+9.2+9.5+9.46=55.06=9.1666…\\frac{9.0+9.1+8.8+9.2+9.5+9.4}{6} =\\frac{55.0}{6} =9.1666\\ldots CMS=917\\boxed{\\mathrm{CMS}=917} \n\nThe dimensional architecture is strongly high-end but not flat: **IC and GR are the clearest spikes; CD is the relative bottleneck**, with AD, ID, and RL clustered closely around 9. The protocol cautions that CMS is only an equal-weight magnitude summary and must not replace the underlying architecture. \n\n**Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)**\n\n**GΩ | 917**\n\nRL: 9.0\n\nAD: 9.1\n\nCD: 8.8\n\nID: 9.2\n\nIC: 9.5\n\nGR: 9.4\n\n**Confidence:** High\n\n**Primary uncertainty:** The principal uncertainty is the G/GΩ boundary: G follows directly from the artifact, whereas GΩ additionally depends on judging modern successor inquiry as structurally unable to dispense with the entropy/capacity problem-framework rather than merely having inherited its vocabulary and conventions.", "## 1. Operational Order (TORC)\n\n# **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**\n\n*A Mathematical Theory of Communication* warrants **GΩ**, rather than merely a very high Level 3 or base G classification.\n\nThe decisive operation is not its mathematical sophistication by itself. The paper identifies an independently intelligible target—**communication under uncertainty and channel limitation**—and relocates the explanatory basis of that target. Instead of treating successful communication principally in terms of particular signals, engineering devices, message meanings, or ad hoc modulation schemes, it makes the **statistical structure of sources, entropy/information, channel capacity, noise, and coding limits** the governing explanatory architecture. Shannon explicitly frames the project as extending earlier communication theory to incorporate noise, statistical structure of messages, and achievable savings. \n\nThis matters under TORC because G requires a change in **what explains the target**, not merely a better technique for calculating or manipulating it. The protocol explicitly says that no amount of complexity or ingenuity establishes G unless the artifact changes the explanatory basis through which an independently identifiable target becomes intelligible. \n\nA particularly strong piece of artifact-internal evidence is the noiseless coding theorem: entropy HH is not merely one useful descriptor among others; it determines the fundamental channel capacity required for efficient coding, with an achievable and impossible side to the result.  The same architecture is then extended to noisy and continuous channels, establishing bounds and capacities in terms of information-theoretic quantities rather than merely particular engineering implementations. \n\n### Boundary analysis\n\n**Why GΩ rather than 3c.** Level 3—even at its highest integrated form—remains a recursive operation within a problem-space. G requires the stronger move of relocating what explains the problem-space. The protocol draws exactly this boundary: “Level 3 can transform consciousness within a problem-space. G transforms what explains the problem-space or target.” \n\nHere, an account of the paper merely as a sophisticated integration of pre-existing communication problems is insufficient. Once entropy, information rate, capacity, coding, and statistical source/channel models are installed, questions such as “how much can this channel reliably transmit?” acquire a different kind of answer. The paper is therefore not merely reflecting on or integrating existing communication practice; it introduces a new explanatory foundation.\n\n### G gate — explicit application\n\n**1. Independent Target Test — PASS.** The target can be stated without using Shannon's framework: reliable communication of signals/messages through channels subject to physical or statistical limitations. It exists independently of the theory. The protocol requires precisely such an independently identifiable target. \n\n**2. Previously unseen generative structure — PASS.** The integrated structure of source entropy/information rate, channel capacity, coding, equivocation/noise, and probabilistic source/channel models supplies the operative mechanism. The paper does not merely rename communication phenomena; mathematical consequences follow from the structure, including attainable rates and impossibility bounds. The coding theorem is especially probative. \n\n**3. Generative-mechanism vs. explanatory-schema test — PASS.** This is not merely a large conceptual picture arranging known facts. Entropy and capacity generate quantitative consequences across distinct communication instances. For example, source statistics determine coding requirements, while capacity places a principled upper limit on transmission rate. That is the protocol's distinction between an explanatory schema and a mechanism that produces nontrivial consequences across instances.\n\n**4. Explanatory Replacement Principle — PASS.** The resulting theory makes an account confined to particular modulation schemes, physical signal forms, or raw symbol frequencies insufficient for the paper's central achievement. The abstraction to source/channel statistics and information quantities is necessary to obtain its general theorems.\n\n**5. Explanatory-Object Firewall — PASS.** The paper certainly improves methods for calculating communication performance, but that is not all it does. It changes the explanatory object from particular signal manipulations to fundamental information-bearing and channel constraints. Thus its achievement is not merely computational or representational optimization.\n\n**6. Computational/Procedural Generativity Firewall — PASS.** Coding procedures occur in the paper, but G does not rest on their procedural novelty. The stronger evidence is the derivation of universal attainable/impossible bounds from entropy and capacity. The theoretical structure explains why coding can succeed below certain rates and why no coding procedure can succeed above them. \n\n**7. Artifact-Internal Generativity Firewall — PASS.** Entropy and channel capacity do not merely organize the paper's own notation. They refer to independently specified stochastic sources and communication channels and generate consequences concerning them.\n\n**8. Fallible Explanation Principle — PASS.** G does not require ultimate truth; it requires externally constrained explanatory success rather than definitional self-confirmation. Shannon's quantities are constrained by independently characterizable source probabilities, bandwidth, noise, signal power, channel transition behavior, and achievable coding performance. They therefore do not succeed solely because the theory defines its own vocabulary.\n\n**9. Lower-order insufficiency — PASS.** Direct representation, symbolic organization, or recursive integration do not explain the central mathematical accomplishment. The artifact's dominant operation is construction of an explanatory foundation capable of deriving limits and possibilities across communication systems. The protocol warns explicitly that magnitude cannot substitute for this operation; here the operation itself is present. \n\nAccordingly, **G is independently established**.\n\n### GΩ gate — explicit application\n\nGΩ adds a separate condition: actual successor inquiry in the native domain must demonstrate that the relocated foundation became a **non-optional baseline**, not merely famous or influential. The protocol requires independent successor evidence for this step. \n\n**Every G condition — PASS**, as above.\n\n**Successor-Baseline Test — PASS.** Adequate modern inquiry into fundamental channel coding limits cannot simply ignore entropy, information rate, channel capacity, and the reliable-transmission boundary and revert to the preceding engineering explanatory organization without losing the ability to formulate the central problem at its modern theoretical level. IEEE's current technical treatment, for example, still defines channel capacity as the maximum reliable information rate and explicitly identifies Shannon's 1948 result as establishing the concept and the below-capacity/above-capacity distinction. \n\n**Baseline-condition transformation — PASS.** Successor theories may alter coding constructions, generalize channel models, replace particular proofs, refine entropy concepts, or introduce different formal machinery, but an adequate theory of fundamental communication limits must still reckon with the distinction between source information, coding, noise, and achievable channel rate.\n\n**Structural rather than reputational necessity — PASS.** This judgment does not depend on citation count, canonical fame, or historical admiration. The necessity follows because successor work asking what rates are achievable or impossible requires an equivalent informational/capacity structure to formulate and answer the question. This is exactly the distinction TORC draws between mere influence and successor-baseline necessity. \n\nTherefore **GΩ is warranted**.\n\n### Why not M\n\nThe next relevant level is **M — Constitutive Framework-Space Constraint**. M is stronger than establishing an unavoidable successor explanatory baseline: the artifact must prove or identify a constraint that **all adequate frameworks of the relevant class must obey**, such that equally adequate alternatives cannot coherently bypass the constraint. \n\nShannon's paper does establish profound impossibility limits *inside communication theory*: for specified source/channel classes, rates above capacity cannot achieve arbitrarily reliable communication. But that is not the same as demonstrating a constitutive restriction upon the **space of admissible explanatory frameworks themselves**. Alternative mathematical formulations can redescribe information and communication while remaining adequate, provided they recover the relevant operational relations or limiting results. The paper establishes an exceptionally strong explanatory framework and unavoidable successor problem structure; it does not demonstrate that all adequate conceptual frameworks for communication must instantiate Shannon's exact framework as a constitutive necessity.\n\nSo the M **Framework-Space Test fails**: an alternative adequate framework can, in principle, represent the same physical/informational constraints using different primitives. Likewise, the Constitutive Subject-Matter Firewall blocks promotion merely because the paper establishes fundamental limits. TORC explicitly distinguishes a fundamental limitation *within a framework/domain* from a constraint on admissible framework-space itself. \n\n---\n\n## 2. Cognitive Magnitude Score (CMS)\n\nThe protocol requires each dimension to be scored independently using integer anchors and decimal interpolation, with the profile locked before calculating CMS. \n\n### **RL — 8.7**\n\n**Bounding anchors: 8–9.**\n\nThe complete 8 anchor requires dense cross-structural dependency in which remote relations constrain one another and simultaneous coordination is central; 9 requires exceptional heterogeneous interdependence across substantially different levels or domains. \n\nThe paper coordinates source statistics, Markov processes, entropy, coding, channel structure, noise, conditional uncertainty, continuous signals, bandwidth, power and probability distributions. Altering definitions such as entropy or capacity propagates through many later theorems. This clearly establishes RL8.\n\nMovement toward 9 is strong because discrete and continuous models, probabilistic sources, engineering channel constraints and coding results mutually interact. The shortfall is that the architecture remains largely within a coherent mathematical-communication ontology rather than coordinating exceptionally heterogeneous intellectual domains. Hence **8.7**, not 9.0.\n\n**Principal evidence:** the repeated interdependence among entropy, source models, channel constraints, coding efficiency and capacity.\n\n### **AD — 9.2**\n\n**Bounding anchors: 9–10.**\n\nThe 9 anchor requires very deep invariant/meta-level structures organizing broad or heterogeneous material with unusually disciplined relation-preservation. \n\nThat anchor is satisfied. The abstraction from physical signals and message contents to probability distributions and information quantities is several layers removed from concrete transmission while preserving quantitative relations. The same abstract architecture covers discrete sources, constrained channels, noise and continuous channels.\n\nMovement toward 10 is present in the exceptional invariance of the framework across representations, but the paper does not obviously exhaust the reference-level abstraction ceiling demanded for 10. **9.2** is therefore warranted.\n\n**Principal evidence:** entropy as a representation-independent measure of source uncertainty and capacity as a generalized limit applying across materially different channel implementations.\n\n### **CD — 8.9**\n\n**Bounding anchors: 8–9.**\n\nCD8 requires heterogeneous requirements interacting across levels such that optimizing locally compromises other requirements; CD9 requires numerous heterogeneous, strongly coupled constraints leaving a very narrow class of successful constructions. \n\nThe paper must simultaneously maintain probabilistic consistency, mathematical tractability, operational interpretation, coding achievability, converse/impossibility results, noise models, bandwidth restrictions, power restrictions and consistency between discrete and continuous cases. Its fundamental results depend on satisfying these jointly.\n\nThis strongly approaches 9. Yet the framework admits many different codes, distributions and channel realizations; the successful *theory* is tightly constrained, but the construction space is not so uniquely compressed that the full 9 anchor is beyond doubt. Hence **8.9**.\n\n**Principal evidence:** coupled requirements linking probability, entropy, capacity, coding, noise and physically meaningful transmission limits.\n\n### **ID — 9.3**\n\n**Bounding anchors: 9–10.**\n\nThe 9 anchor requires exceptional inferential reconstruction in which substantial relevant information still leaves the successful relationship deeply underdetermined and highly non-obvious even to a strong domain reasoner. \n\nThe paper crosses precisely that kind of gap. Existing observations about bandwidth, modulation and statistical message structure do not routinely yield the identification of entropy with information rate, the conception of channel capacity as an optimization quantity, or the coding theorem's paired achievability/impossibility result. Those are structural reconstructions, not long chains of routine calculations.\n\nThere is demonstrable movement beyond 9, but 10 requires the protocol's reference-level extreme. The artifact alone does not compel that ceiling judgment. Thus **9.3**.\n\n**Principal evidence:** the inferential passage from concrete communication-engineering problems to general probabilistic limits on reliable coding.\n\n### **IC — 9.4**\n\n**Bounding anchors: 9–10.**\n\nIC9 requires very large heterogeneous complexity to be reorganized through a remarkably economical architecture while preserving fine structure and enabling extensive reconstruction or derivation. \n\nThis anchor is fully met. A comparatively small set of primitives—probability distributions, entropy, information rate, equivocation, capacity, coding—organizes an unusually broad collection of communication phenomena. From that architecture, substantial results can be reconstructed or derived: coding efficiencies, rate limits, noise penalties and channel capacities.\n\nThe architecture also moves materially toward the 10 ceiling because the compression ratio is exceptional: enormous families of physically different communication systems become analyzable through the same compact conceptual machinery. But the complete reference-level 10 anchor is not sufficiently evidenced to assign 10. **9.4**.\n\n**Principal evidence:** the compact entropy/capacity/coding architecture and its extensive reconstructive mathematical power.\n\n### **GR — 9.0**\n\n**Bounding anchors: 9–10.**\n\nGR9 requires the same structure to work across exceptionally broad and structurally distant problem-spaces while retaining fine structural identity with little ad hoc modification.  The protocol also warns that mere broad applicability or repeated use within one narrow ontology is not enough. \n\nThe 9 anchor is met at its lower edge. Within the artifact, essentially the same informational architecture transfers from discrete sources to finite-state sources, constrained channels, noisy channels and continuous channels. These are markedly heterogeneous mathematical/physical cases, yet the central relations survive.\n\nI would not move appreciably toward 10 on artifact-internal evidence alone. The theory has identifiable boundaries, and transfer into domains outside communication/information problems often requires substantive reinterpretation. **9.0** therefore preserves the protocol's applicability/transfer firewall.\n\n**Principal evidence:** transfer of entropy/capacity reasoning across discrete, stochastic, noisy and continuous communication systems.\n\n### Committed profile\n\n**RL / AD / CD / ID / IC / GR: 8.7 / 9.2 / 8.9 / 9.3 / 9.4 / 9.0**\n\nProfile locked.\n\nThe protocol gives all six dimensions equal weight and specifies CMS as the mean of the committed scores multiplied by 100, rounded to the nearest whole integer. \n\n8.7+9.2+8.9+9.3+9.4+9.06=54.56=9.08333…\\frac{8.7+9.2+8.9+9.3+9.4+9.0}{6} =\\frac{54.5}{6} =9.08333\\ldots CMS=908\\boxed{\\text{CMS}=908} \n\nThe profile is high and relatively balanced, with its strongest feature in **integrative compression**, followed closely by inferential distance and abstraction depth. Relational load is somewhat lower—not because the theory is simple, but because TORC's RL firewall prevents mathematical coupling inside one comparatively coherent domain from being mistaken for maximal heterogeneous relational span. \n\n**Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)**\n\n**GΩ | 908**\n\nRL: 8.7\n\nAD: 9.2\n\nCD: 8.9\n\nID: 9.3\n\nIC: 9.4\n\nGR: 9.0\n\n**Confidence:** High\n\n**Primary uncertainty:** The main uncertainty is the exact GΩ/M boundary: Shannon establishes extraordinarily strong domain-level impossibility constraints, but I do not find sufficient evidence that the artifact constrains the space of all admissible explanatory frameworks, rather than establishing an unavoidable explanatory baseline within communication theory.", "## 1. Operational Order (TORC)\n\n# **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**\n\nThe paper warrants **GΩ**, not merely because it is mathematically sophisticated or historically famous, but because its dominant operation changes the explanatory basis of communication itself.\n\nShannon starts from an independently identifiable target: **reliable reproduction of a selected message across a communication system, including under noise**.  He then relocates the problem from the semantic content of messages to the statistical structure of selections among possible messages, sources, channels, and received signals. From this relocation he develops a compact generative architecture involving entropy, equivocation/conditional uncertainty, information rate, channel capacity, redundancy, and coding limits. The paper explicitly extends the framework across discrete sources, discrete noisy channels, continuous sources/channels, and Gaussian noise. \n\nMost importantly, the framework generates nontrivial consequences rather than merely redescribing communication. Shannon derives the entropy functional from structural conditions, defines capacity through maximization of transmitted information, and establishes coding results separating achievable from impossible transmission rates. For continuous additive-noise channels, for example, the same architecture yields capacity as the maximized difference between received-signal entropy and noise entropy. \n\nThat satisfies the protocol's G requirements: a previously unseen generative structure, relocation of the explanatory foundation, successful organization of the domain, and an operation not adequately described as ordinary representation, reflection, or recursive reorganization. \n\n### Boundary: why G rather than Level 3\n\nLevel 3 would remain a recursive transformation **within** an operative explanatory framework. That is insufficient here. Shannon does not merely optimize existing telecommunications mathematics or synthesize Nyquist/Hartley/Wiener. The paper changes what counts as the explanatory variables of the problem: message-selection probabilities become measurable information; statistical dependence becomes transmitted information; uncertainty becomes entropy; and noisy communication acquires a limiting capacity independent of any particular coding implementation.\n\nThe strongest evidence is the coding-theorem architecture. The result is not simply a better communication technique. It establishes that below a mathematically defined capacity appropriate codes exist with arbitrarily small error, whereas above the capacity reliable transmission cannot in general be obtained.  That is explanatory relocation with generative consequences, exactly what the protocol distinguishes from mere complexity or integration.\n\n### G gate\n\n**Independent Target Test — PASS.**\n\nThe target can be stated without Shannon's framework: determine the achievable limits of reproducing messages through communication systems, particularly in the presence of noise. Shannon states essentially this problem before constructing the machinery. \n\n**Previously unseen generative structure — PASS.**\n\nThe entropy/information/capacity/coding architecture generates quantitative limits and constructive existence results across multiple communication cases.\n\n**Explanatory foundation relocated — PASS.**\n\nThe central explanatory object becomes statistical selection and uncertainty rather than semantic meaning or particular signal technology.\n\n**Relocated foundation successfully organizes the domain — PASS.**\n\nThe same architecture coordinates sources, coding, noisy and noiseless channels, continuous channels, bandwidth, noise and transmission rates.\n\n**Levels 1–3 insufficient — PASS.**\n\nThe operation is not adequately characterized as representation, reflective manipulation, or recursive reorganization of an existing communication framework.\n\nThus **G is established artifact-internally** under the protocol. \n\n### GΩ successor-baseline gate\n\nThe protocol requires something extra here: actual successor inquiry must show that the relocation became a **non-optional epistemic baseline**, rather than merely an influential theory. \n\n**PASS.** Modern classical information theory continues to formulate source compression in terms of Shannon entropy and noisy transmission in terms of channel capacity and Shannon's coding theorem. Contemporary courses treat entropy, mutual information, typicality, source coding and channel capacity as the machinery through which the field's central problems are formulated and solved.  Even contemporary coding-theory foundations continue to formulate theoretical limits through Shannon entropy, mutual information and channel capacity. \n\nThe important point is structural rather than reputational: an adequate successor theory of classical reliable communication cannot simply return to treating noise, redundancy and coding efficiency without an equivalent account of information rate and achievable transmission limits. It may generalize or reformulate Shannon's quantities, but it must reckon with the transformed problem. That meets the protocol's successor-baseline criterion. \n\n### Why not M?\n\n**M is not established.**\n\nThe protocol's M gate is stronger: the artifact must demonstrate a constraint on the **space of admissible frameworks themselves**, such that equally adequate frameworks cannot evade it. \n\nShannon proves extremely strong limits **inside specified probabilistic communication models**. For example, capacity constrains every coding scheme satisfying the relevant channel assumptions. But that is not sufficient evidence that every adequate conceptual or formal framework for communication must instantiate Shannon's particular framework-space structure. Indeed, later work can change the channel ontology, admissible resources, error criterion, asymptotics, or information type while retaining adequacy for its new target. The existence of quantum channels with several distinct capacities is a particularly clear illustration that the classical single-capacity architecture is not a constitutive constraint on every adequate communication framework. \n\nSo the strongest warranted classification is **GΩ**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Cognitive Magnitude Score\n\nThe protocol requires the six dimensions to be committed independently before CMS is calculated and forbids adjusting them afterward to make the aggregate look appropriate. \n\n| DimensionScoreArtifact-specific basis |         |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **RL**                                | **9.0** | Exceptional interdependence among source statistics, entropy, coding, redundancy, channel transition probabilities, equivocation, noise, capacity, bandwidth and continuous/discrete representations. The paper repeatedly carries the same relationships across substantially different mathematical levels. **Principal evidence:** source→transmitter→channel→receiver architecture; discrete/continuous/noisy-channel derivations.                                                                                                 |\n| **AD**                                | **9.2** | The paper moves from physical communication systems to stochastic ensembles, entropy rates, conditional uncertainty and invariant limiting quantities while maintaining correspondence back to achievable transmission. It fully satisfies the protocol's 9-anchor of deep invariant/meta-level organization; movement above 9 comes from the unusually disciplined passage between physical, probabilistic and asymptotic descriptions. **Principal evidence:** entropy axiomatization and generalized channel-capacity construction. |\n| **CD**                                | **8.8** | Successful results simultaneously respect probability laws, source statistics, code structure, rates, channel transition behavior, error/equivocation, power/bandwidth constraints and asymptotic limits. This approaches exceptional solution-space compression, but some sections operate under deliberately specialized assumptions, so a full 9 is not forced. **Principal evidence:** coding theorems and continuous/noisy-channel analyses.                                                                                      |\n| **ID**                                | **9.3** | The transition from telecommunications engineering problems to a general statistical measure of information and then to existence/impossibility theorems for reliable coding is profoundly underdetermined by routine procedure. The paper itself acknowledges Nyquist/Hartley and related foundations, preventing attribution of the entire conceptual distance to an unconstrained leap. **Principal evidence:** entropy→information rate→capacity→coding-theorem chain.                                                             |\n| **IC**                                | **9.5** | An exceptionally economical architecture organizes a very large heterogeneous field: source uncertainty, redundancy, coding, noise, continuous signals, discrete signals, bandwidth and achievable reliability become recoverable through a small family of quantities and relationships. This is arguably the profile's strongest dimension. **Principal evidence:** entropy and conditional entropy feeding directly into information rate and capacity across the paper's major cases.                                              |\n| **GR**                                | **9.1** | The same core architecture transfers with high fidelity from discrete noiseless sources to Markov sources, noisy channels and continuous channels, including Gaussian cases. This is demonstrated *inside the artifact*, rather than inferred merely from later applications. **Principal evidence:** the explicit progression from discrete to continuous communication theory.                                                                                                                                                       |\n\nThe high-end scores follow the protocol's anchors: 9 requires exceptional heterogeneous interdependence for RL, exceptional disciplined abstraction for AD, exceptional solution-space compression for CD, exceptional inferential reconstruction for ID, exceptional economical organization with reconstructive power for IC, and exceptional structurally faithful transfer for GR.      \n\n**Committed profile:**\n\n**9.0 / 9.2 / 8.8 / 9.3 / 9.5 / 9.1**\n\nProfile locked.\n\nThe protocol specifies equal weighting: take the mean of the six committed decimal scores and multiply by 100. \n\n9.0+9.2+8.8+9.3+9.5+9.16×100=915\\frac{9.0+9.2+8.8+9.3+9.5+9.1}{6}\\times100 =915 \n\n**CMS = 915**\n\nThe architecture is unusually balanced at the extreme high end, with **Integrative Compression** the clearest peak and **Constraint Density** the relative bottleneck. That distinction matters: the score is not being raised because the paper is famous or because TORC is GΩ; the protocol expressly prohibits both forms of leakage. \n\n---\n\n**Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)**\n\n**GΩ | 915**\n\nRL: 9.0\n\nAD: 9.2\n\nCD: 8.8\n\nID: 9.3\n\nIC: 9.5\n\nGR: 9.1\n\n**Confidence:** Moderate\n\n**Primary uncertainty:** The principal uncertainty is the GΩ/M boundary: Shannon's coding limits impose universal constraints within specified classical channel models, but the artifact does not establish strongly enough that its structure is an inescapable constraint on the space of all equally adequate communication frameworks, as M requires."];
export const consensusReport="## 1. Seven evaluator results\n\nThe seven reports yield the following committed evaluations. The first row is the evaluation included directly in your message; the remaining six are the attached reports. The attached profiles are reproduced from their committed DIP sections.      \n\n| EvaluatorTORCCMSRLADCDIDICGRConfidence |        |         |     |     |     |     |     |     |          |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------ | ------- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | -------- |\n| 1                                      | **GΩ** | **905** | 8.5 | 9.0 | 9.1 | 9.2 | 9.4 | 9.1 | Moderate |\n| 2                                      | **GΩ** | **898** | 8.8 | 9.2 | 8.9 | 9.1 | 9.3 | 8.6 | High     |\n| 3                                      | **M**  | **887** | 8.8 | 9.1 | 8.7 | 9.0 | 9.3 | 8.3 | Moderate |\n| 4                                      | **GΩ** | **918** | 8.8 | 9.1 | 9.0 | 9.4 | 9.6 | 9.2 | Moderate |\n| 5                                      | **GΩ** | **917** | 9.0 | 9.1 | 8.8 | 9.2 | 9.5 | 9.4 | High     |\n| 6                                      | **GΩ** | **908** | 8.7 | 9.2 | 8.9 | 9.3 | 9.4 | 9.0 | High     |\n| 7                                      | **GΩ** | **915** | 9.0 | 9.2 | 8.8 | 9.3 | 9.5 | 9.1 | Moderate |\n\n## 2. Numerical aggregation\n\nStandard deviation is reported as the **population standard deviation across the complete seven-report dataset**.\n\n| MeasureMeanMedianMinimumMaximumRangeSD |           |         |     |     |     |      |\n| -------------------------------------- | --------- | ------- | --- | --- | --- | ---- |\n| **CMS**                                | **906.9** | **908** | 887 | 918 | 31  | 10.5 |\n| **RL**                                 | 8.80      | **8.8** | 8.5 | 9.0 | 0.5 | 0.16 |\n| **AD**                                 | 9.13      | **9.1** | 9.0 | 9.2 | 0.2 | 0.07 |\n| **CD**                                 | 8.89      | **8.9** | 8.7 | 9.1 | 0.4 | 0.12 |\n| **ID**                                 | 9.21      | **9.2** | 9.0 | 9.4 | 0.4 | 0.12 |\n| **IC**                                 | 9.43      | **9.4** | 9.3 | 9.6 | 0.3 | 0.10 |\n| **GR**                                 | 8.96      | **9.1** | 8.3 | 9.4 | 1.1 | 0.35 |\n\nThe numerical convergence is strong. Five of the six dimensions have evaluator ranges of only 0.2–0.5. **GR is the clear exception**, with a 1.1-point range caused by different judgments about whether transfer among discrete, continuous, noisy, Gaussian, and related information-theoretic settings constitutes sufficiently distant structural transfer for the 9-range.\n\n## 3. TORC consensus\n\nThe seven TORC classifications are:\n\n**GΩ, GΩ, M, GΩ, GΩ, GΩ, GΩ**\n\nFrequency:\n\n- **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity:** 6/7 \n- **M — Constitutive Consciousness / Framework-Space Constraint:** 1/7 \n\nThe modal classification is therefore **GΩ**, with **85.7%** of evaluators independently selecting it. The **consensus TORC is GΩ**.\n\n**TORC agreement: High**, but with one material disagreement at precisely the protocol's important **GΩ → M boundary**.\n\nAll seven reports substantially agree on the underlying G operation. The recurrent claim is that the paper does more than offer mathematical tools for an already-fixed communications problem: it relocates the explanatory basis toward source statistics, uncertainty/information, entropy, coding, noise, channel capacity, and achievable or impossible transmission rates. The target—communication or reliable transmission through constrained/noisy channels—is treated as independently identifiable rather than as something created by the theory itself.   \n\nThe six GΩ reports additionally converge on **successor-baseline necessity**: later adequate inquiry into fundamental communication limits may revise models, proofs, primitives, or particular quantities, but cannot simply return to a pre-relocation organization that omits equivalent questions about information quantity, coding, noise, and achievable transmission rates. Those reports explicitly distinguish this structural necessity from fame, citation, or influence.   \n\n### The GΩ → M disagreement\n\nThe disagreement is unusually precise.\n\nThe **M evaluator** argues that Shannon's converse coding results already satisfy the Framework-Space Test because the entropy/capacity bounds constrain *any coding scheme* satisfying the stated source/channel models. Changing the particular code cannot escape the achievable/impossible boundary; individual codes are variables inside the constrained space. On that interpretation, the paper establishes not merely a successor baseline but constitutive constraints on the admissible class of communication systems. \n\nThe **GΩ majority** applies a wider interpretation of “framework-space.” It distinguishes a universal bound over codes or communication systems **within specified probabilistic assumptions** from a constraint governing all equally adequate explanatory or conceptual frameworks for communication. These reports argue that alternative frameworks can change primitives, channel ontology, resources, error criteria, or formal representation while recovering the relevant phenomena; therefore Shannon establishes fundamental domain-level limits without demonstrating the stronger meta-level impossibility required for M.   \n\nThat **Framework-Space Test** is the primary and only material TORC disagreement.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Consensus analysis\n\n## A. Operational Order rationale\n\nThe consensus classification is **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**.\n\nAcross the reports, the recurring artifact-specific operation is an **explanatory relocation of communication**. Particular messages, signal forms, semantic meanings, modulation techniques, and individual devices cease to be the general explanatory foundation. In their place, the paper constructs an architecture involving probabilistic sources, entropy/information, coding, conditional uncertainty or equivocation, channel statistics, noise, capacity, and limiting transmission rates.\n\nThe evaluators repeatedly stress that these objects are not merely descriptive conveniences. They generate consequences: source uncertainty constrains compression; channel structure constrains reliable transmission; coding theorems separate achievable from impossible rates; and the same architecture coordinates noiseless, noisy, discrete, and continuous cases. Thus the common evaluator judgment is that Levels 1–3 are insufficient because the dominant achievement is not representation or recursion *within* an existing explanatory space but a change in **what explains the target domain**.  \n\nThe Ω increment is also supported by six reports. They judge the relocated problem structure to have become non-optional for adequate successor inquiry: later theories may reformulate entropy, alter channel models, introduce new coding constructions, or enlarge the domain, but fundamental inquiry into information transmission must still reckon with equivalents of information quantity, coding, channel limitation, noise, and achievable rates.  \n\n## B. Boundary analysis\n\n### Why GΩ rather than G\n\nBase **G** is effectively unanimous. The reports agree that the artifact relocates the explanatory foundation of an independently identifiable target and generates nontrivial consequences from the replacement architecture.\n\nThe step to **GΩ** rests on a separate claim: that the relocated explanatory problem became a **successor baseline** rather than merely one successful framework among optional competitors. Six evaluators affirm this. Their recurrent reasoning is not that later researchers cite Shannon, but that successor work concerned with fundamental communication limits cannot discard the transformed questions themselves without explanatory loss. \n\nThere is some acknowledged uncertainty about exactly how strict this historical/structural necessity should be. Several GΩ evaluators identify the Ω gate itself as a principal uncertainty. But none of the seven ultimately assigns base G, so that uncertainty does not produce an actual classification split.\n\n### Why not M\n\nThis is the contested ceiling.\n\nThe majority holds that **fundamental impossibility bounds on communication systems are not automatically framework-space impossibility bounds**. Shannon's results constrain codes under stated source/channel models, but, on the majority interpretation, the paper does not establish that every adequate alternative explanatory framework for communication must instantiate the same constitutive architecture. Hence the explanatory baseline is successor-necessary without being demonstrated as a universal constraint on the construction of adequate frameworks themselves. \n\nThe M dissent argues that this distinction is too restrictive: if all alternative codes satisfying the relevant communication model remain subject to entropy/capacity converse bounds, then those bounds already delimit the admissible framework class. \n\nThe 6–1 distribution therefore supports **GΩ**, while preserving a serious minority argument for M.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Six-dimensional synthesis\n\nThe **median** is used below as the consensus dimensional result, while the mean and complete distribution remain available in the aggregation table.\n\n### RL — Relational Load\n\n**Consensus: 8.8**\n\n**Observed range: 8.5–9.0**\n\nEvery evaluator identifies dense interdependence among source distributions, entropy, redundancy, coding, channel transition behavior, noise/equivocation, capacity, transmission rate, and discrete/continuous formulations. Changes to source or channel structure propagate into coding limits and capacity results; the major parts therefore cannot be understood as independent mathematical modules.\n\nThe shared restraint is that this exceptionally dense network remains concentrated within a relatively unified information/communication ontology. Evaluators therefore stop around the 8–9 boundary rather than inferring maximal relational span from mathematical coupling alone.  \n\n**Material disagreement:** None. The 0.5 range is small.\n\n### AD — Abstraction Depth\n\n**Consensus: 9.1**\n\n**Observed range: 9.0–9.2**\n\nThis is the most numerically stable dimension. The reports consistently emphasize the move from concrete messages, waveforms, devices, and channels to probability distributions, stochastic sources, entropy, abstract channel relations, capacity, and asymptotic transmission limits, while retaining correspondence back to concrete communication performance.\n\nThe same abstraction survives shifts among discrete, continuous, noiseless, and noisy cases. That disciplined preservation of relations across multiple abstraction levels is why every evaluator places AD at 9.0–9.2.  \n\n**Material disagreement:** None.\n\n### CD — Constraint Density\n\n**Consensus: 8.9**\n\n**Observed range: 8.7–9.1**\n\nThe reports converge on a strongly coupled construction problem: probability laws, definitions of information, source statistics, coding relations, channel transition structures, noise, asymptotic arguments, achievability and converse results, and—in continuous settings—bandwidth and power restrictions must remain mutually consistent.\n\nThe difference between the high-8 and low-9 evaluations concerns how narrow the successful construction space really is. Some evaluators emphasize the severe mutual constraints among definitions and theorems; others emphasize the availability of alternative formulations, proof strategies, channel classes, and coding constructions. \n\n**Material disagreement:** Minor; it concerns interpolation around the 9 anchor, not the underlying evidence.\n\n### ID — Inferential Distance\n\n**Consensus: 9.2**\n\n**Observed range: 9.0–9.4**\n\nAll reports treat the decisive reasoning leap as highly non-routine: starting from concrete engineering problems of signaling, modulation, noise, and message reproduction and arriving at a probabilistic measure of information, entropy-based source limits, optimization-defined channel capacity, and existence/impossibility theorems.\n\nSeveral evaluators specifically emphasize that the coding-theorem architecture is not the predictable result of applying a routine mathematical procedure to the prior problem statement. The variation from 9.0 to 9.4 reflects degree, not a disagreement over whether the inferential leap reaches the 9-range.  \n\n**Material disagreement:** None beyond interpolation.\n\n### IC — Integrative Compression\n\n**Consensus: 9.4**\n\n**Observed range: 9.3–9.6**\n\nThis is the most consistently identified **profile peak**. A small family of concepts—probability, entropy/information, conditional uncertainty, coding, and capacity—organizes a very large range of source, channel, noise, redundancy, rate, and continuous/discrete problems while preserving enough structure to derive quantitative consequences.\n\nThe evaluators repeatedly distinguish this from simplification by deletion: the compact architecture retains reconstructive and theorem-generating power. Every score falls between 9.3 and 9.6.  \n\n**Material disagreement:** None.\n\n### GR — Generalization Reach\n\n**Consensus: 9.1**\n\n**Observed range: 8.3–9.4**\n\nAll reports agree that the architecture transfers across significantly different communication settings: discrete and continuous sources, noisy and noiseless channels, different source statistics, different physical realizations, constrained channels, and related coding problems.\n\nThis is the **largest dimensional disagreement**. The conservative evaluations argue that these cases, though heterogeneous, remain inside one overarching information/communication ontology and therefore should not automatically satisfy the strict GR9 requirement for structurally distant transfer. The higher evaluations judge the discrete/continuous, noisy/noiseless, Gaussian, constrained, and related transitions sufficiently structurally varied—and sufficiently faithful in preservation of the core architecture—to clear GR9.   \n\n**Material disagreement:** Yes. This is the largest numerical disagreement in the dimensional profile.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Final CMS\n\n**Mean CMS: 906.9**\n\n**Median CMS: 908**\n\nThe seven CMS evaluations range from **887 to 918**, a spread of 31 points, with a population standard deviation of **10.5**.\n\nFollowing the requested protocol, the **median CMS of 908** is the headline consensus CMS.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Evaluator agreement\n\n**TORC agreement: High**\n\nSix of seven evaluators independently assign **GΩ**. The one dissent is operationally important because it crosses the GΩ→M boundary, but it does not undermine the existence of a clear modal consensus.\n\n**CMS stability: High**\n\nThe scores cluster closely around \\~907 despite independently produced profiles: the full range is only 31 points on the 1000-point CMS scale, and the standard deviation is 10.5. The M evaluator actually supplies the lowest CMS, reinforcing that TORC classification and numerical magnitude were not being mechanically coupled.\n\n**Dimensional stability: High**\n\nFive dimensions have ranges of 0.2–0.5 and very small standard deviations. The main exception is **GR**, with a 1.1 range. Its disagreement arises from different applications of the protocol's domain-distance/generalization firewall: whether transfer across materially different communication models counts as exceptional cross-problem-space generalization or remains transfer within one unified information-theoretic ontology.\n\n---\n\n## Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)\n\n**GΩ | 908**\n\n**Mean CMS:** 906.9\n\n**Median CMS:** 908\n\n**RL:** 8.8\n\n**AD:** 9.1\n\n**CD:** 8.9\n\n**ID:** 9.2\n\n**IC:** 9.4\n\n**GR:** 9.1\n\n**TORC agreement:** High\n\n**CMS stability:** High\n\n**Dimensional stability:** High\n\n**Evaluator distribution:** GΩ, GΩ, M, GΩ, GΩ, GΩ, GΩ\n\n**Primary point of disagreement:** Whether Shannon's converse coding limits constitute constraints only on communication systems within specified probabilistic models, supporting GΩ, or constrain the admissible framework-space itself strongly enough to satisfy M.\n\n# Consensus Evaluation\n\n*A Mathematical Theory of Communication* receives a consensus classification of **GΩ — Generative Consciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity**, with a **median CMS of 908**. Six of seven independent evaluators assign GΩ; one assigns M. The numerical evaluation is likewise highly stable, with all seven CMS scores falling between 887 and 918.\n\nThe strongest point of agreement is the paper's **generative operation**. Across the reports, its central achievement is not characterized merely as mathematical sophistication, a new calculation method, or an especially effective engineering technique. The paper relocates the explanatory basis of communication from particular messages, signals, meanings, devices, and modulation schemes toward a general probabilistic architecture involving source uncertainty, entropy or information, coding, noise, equivocation, channel capacity, and achievable transmission rates. Those quantities do explanatory work: they generate compression limits, reliable-transmission bounds, and achievability or impossibility results across different communication systems.\n\nThis satisfies the G threshold because the target exists independently of the theory—the reliable transmission and reconstruction of messages through constrained or noisy channels—while the theory changes what explains that target. The evaluators consistently judge ordinary representational or recursive descriptions insufficient: the paper installs a new explanatory foundation from which substantive consequences follow.\n\nThe consensus advances from G to **GΩ** because six evaluators also find successor-baseline necessity. Their shared argument is structural rather than reputational. Later theories can revise assumptions, alter channel models, devise new codes, generalize entropy, change proof techniques, or introduce different formal machinery, but adequate inquiry into fundamental information transmission cannot simply return to a pre-Shannon organization in which information quantity, source statistics, coding, noise, and achievable communication limits disappear as explanatory problems. The particular formalism can change; the transformed problem-space cannot be discarded without explanatory loss.\n\nThe sole TORC dissent concerns the next boundary, **GΩ versus M**. The M evaluation emphasizes Shannon's converse theorems: within the relevant source and channel models, no alternative coding scheme can escape the entropy and capacity bounds. On that view, the theory does more than establish an unavoidable explanatory baseline; it delimits the admissible space of communication systems themselves.\n\nThe six-report majority adopts a stricter interpretation of M's Framework-Space Test. It distinguishes a universal impossibility result over codes satisfying a specified communication model from a constitutive limitation on the space of all equally adequate explanatory frameworks. Shannon's results establish fundamental boundaries within broad classes of communication systems, but the paper does not demonstrate that every adequate alternative framework for communication must employ the same primitives or instantiate the same framework architecture. On that criterion, the proper classification remains GΩ rather than M.\n\nThe dimensional profile is strikingly stable. **Integrative Compression**, with a consensus score of **9.4**, is the clearest peak. A compact family of concepts—probability, entropy and related information quantities, coding, channel structure, and capacity—organizes a very large body of otherwise heterogeneous material while retaining enough structure to derive detailed quantitative consequences. This is compression with reconstructive power rather than simplification by omission.\n\n**Inferential Distance (9.2)** and **Abstraction Depth (9.1)** are similarly exceptional. The evaluators converge on the non-routine transition from concrete communication-engineering problems to abstract statistical measures of uncertainty and then to asymptotic coding limits and achievable/impossible transmission regions. The abstraction remains rigorously connected to concrete systems while spanning discrete, continuous, noisy, and noiseless formulations.\n\n**Constraint Density (8.9)** reflects the requirement that probability, source statistics, information measures, coding, channel behavior, noise, asymptotics, achievability, converse results, and physical restrictions remain jointly coherent. **Relational Load (8.8)** is also very high, although evaluators consistently stop short of the extreme ceiling because the dense dependencies remain concentrated within a relatively unified mathematical and communication-theoretic domain.\n\nThe largest dimensional disagreement concerns **Generalization Reach**, whose scores range from 8.3 to 9.4, with a consensus value of **9.1**. Every evaluator recognizes substantial structural transfer across discrete and continuous sources, noisy and noiseless channels, differing source statistics, channel types, and physical realizations. The disagreement is narrower: whether that breadth constitutes exceptional transfer across sufficiently distant problem-spaces, or remains extraordinarily broad variation inside a single information-theoretic ontology.\n\nTaken together, the seven evaluations describe an artifact whose demonstrated intelligence is not simply high in magnitude but unusually coherent in architecture. Its central accomplishment is a compact generative theory that reorganizes how a pre-existing domain is explained, derives deep limits and possibilities from that reorganization, and establishes a transformed problem structure that successor inquiry cannot adequately ignore. The resulting consensus profile is **GΩ | 908**.";
